data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR works using a guide RNA that binds to target DNA and Cas9, a protein that cuts the DNA at that location [1].\n- Scientists can deliver Cas9 with a synthetic guide RNA to cut the genome at a chosen site, allowing genes to be removed or new ones added [6].\n- It is commonly used to \"knock out\" a gene by breaking it so the cell stops making the protein it codes for [2].\n- New DNA fragments can serve as repair templates, so a disease-causing mutation can be replaced with a healthy sequence [2].\n- It is used to create cell and animal models for research into diseases such as cancer and mental illness, and is being developed as a rapid diagnostic [3]."}

